  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates the challenge and goal of developing bioorthogonal chemistry: reactions that work in a simple flask must be refined so they still operate selectively inside living cells. On the left, a labeled “target” molecule in solution carries a reactive handle, while nearby functional groups such as amines and thiols are shown as potentially competing species. A star-like probe appears to react with the target under relatively controlled chemical conditions.\n\nIn the center, circular arrows and the phrase “iterative refinement of bioorthogonal chemistries” emphasize that this is an optimization process rather than a single step. The chemistry is repeatedly improved to become more compatible with biological environments.\n\nOn the right, the same basic labeling concept is placed inside a cell crowded with organelles, water, and many endogenous reactive groups and ions, including ammonium-like, thiolate, bicarbonate, and carboxylate species. Despite this complex background, the probe still appears to find and label the intended target. Overall, the image conveys the progression from test-tube reactivity to selective intracellular labeling, highlighting the need for chemistries that remain fast and specific in the presence of abundant biological molecules.",
